    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks, that's a relief to hear i'm not just making it up. so if they're like an \"infestation,\" what is the actual process of them hiding in the cracks? like, how do they get under the surface of the artery wall in the first place? it feels like they're specifically looking for ways to get out of sight so the body doesn't notice them right away.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The process is quite insidious, like a slow-motion siege against the inner lining of your arteries, which is called the endothelium. These LDL particles, when they are oxidized or otherwise damaged, become less like harmless delivery trucks and more like unwelcome guests that are allowed to slip through tiny gaps in the artery wall. They don't usually burst through the wall immediately; instead, they get trapped in the sub-intimal space, which is the \"crack\" you mentioned.\n\nThis trapping is where the real trouble starts, as the body's immune response kicks in, mistaking these trapped particles for invaders—a perfect setup for inflammation. It's a slow, creeping process, much like a colony of tiny, persistent burrowing creatures.",
